     iissccssii..ccoonnff — iSCSI initiator configuration file

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The iissccssii..ccoonnff configuration file is used by the iscsictl(8) utility.
     The general syntax is:

             # this is a comment
             nickname_1 {
                variable = value;
                ...
             }
             nickname_2 {
                variable = value;
                ...
             }
             ...


     AAuutthhMMeetthhoodd      Sets the authentication type.  Type can be either "_N_o_n_e",
                     or "_C_H_A_P".  Default is "_N_o_n_e".  When set to CCHHAAPP, both
                     cchhaappIINNaammee and cchhaappSSeeccrreett must be defined.

     cchhaappIINNaammee       Login for CHAP authentication.

     cchhaappSSeeccrreett      Secret for CHAP authentication.

     ttggttCChhaappNNaammee     Target login for Mutual CHAP authentication.

     ttggttCChhaappSSeeccrreett   Target secret for Mutual CHAP authentication.

     HHeeaaddeerrDDiiggeesstt    Sets the header digest; a checksum calculated over the
                     header of iSCSI PDUs, and verified on receive.  Digest
                     can be either "_N_o_n_e", or "_C_R_C_3_2_C".  Default is "_N_o_n_e".

     DDaattaaDDiiggeesstt      Sets the data digest; a checksum calculated over the Data
                     Section of iSCSI PDUs, and verified on receive.  Digest
                     can be either "_N_o_n_e", or "_C_R_C_3_2_C".  Default is "_N_o_n_e".

     IInniittiiaattoorrNNaammee   Sets the initiator name.  By default, the name is con‐
                     catenation of "_i_q_n_._1_9_9_4_-_0_9_._o_r_g_._f_r_e_e_b_s_d_:" with the host‐
                     name.

     TTaarrggeettNNaammee      Sets the target name.  Not required for discovery ses‐
                     sions.

     TTaarrggeettAAddddrreessss   Sets the target address and port, in aaddddrreessss[[::ppoorrtt]] for‐
                     mat.  The aaddddrreessss can be either an IP address, or host‐
                     name.  The optional port defaults to 3260.

     SSeessssiioonnTTyyppee     Sets the session type.  Type can be either "_D_i_s_c_o_v_e_r_y",
                     or "_N_o_r_m_a_l".  Default is "_N_o_r_m_a_l".  For normal sessions,
                     the TTaarrggeettNNaammee must be defined.  Discovery sessions re‐
                     sult in the initiator connecting to all the targets re‐
                     turned by SendTargets iSCSI discovery with the defined
                     TTaarrggeettAAddddrreessss.

     EEnnaabbllee          Enable or disable the session.  State can be either "_O_n",
                     or "_O_f_f".  Default is "_O_n".

     OOffffllooaadd         Name of selected iSCSI hardware offload driver.  Default
                     is "_N_o_n_e".

     PPrroottooccooll        Name of selected protocol.  It can be either "_i_S_E_R", for
                     iSCSI over RDMA, or "_i_S_C_S_I".  Default is "_i_S_C_S_I".

     ddssccpp            The DiffServ Codepoint used for sending data.  The DSCP
                     can be set to numeric, or hexadecimal values directly, as
                     well as the well-defined "_c_s_<_n_>" and "_a_f_<_x_x_>" codepoints.
                     Default is no specified dscp codepoint, which means the
                     default of the outgoing interface is used.

     ppccpp             The 802.1Q Priority CodePoint used for sending packets.
                     The PCP can be set to a value in the range between "_0" to
                     "_7".  When omitted, the default for the outgoing inter‐
                     face is used.

     PPiinnggTTiimmeeoouutt     Specify the time in seconds to wait between pings (SCSI
                     NOP), and for a ping response before declaring the ses‐
                     sion as dead and attempting a re-establishment.  If this
                     entry is not present in the conf file, the default value
                     configured using "_k_e_r_n_._i_s_c_s_i_._p_i_n_g___t_i_m_e_o_u_t" (default at
                     "_5" seconds) is taken by the driver.  If present, the
                     PingTimeout can be set to any positive value starting
                     with "_1".

     LLooggiinnTTiimmeeoouutt    Specify the time in seconds to wait for a login PDU to be
                     sent or received after trying to establish a new session.
                     When no login PDU is received within this time, the login
                     on a particular connection fails and a new reconnection
                     attempt is made.  If this entry is not present in the
                     conf file, the default value of "_6_0" seconds is used, as
                     configured by "_k_e_r_n_._i_s_c_s_i_._l_o_g_i_n___t_i_m_e_o_u_t".  The LoginTime‐
                     out can be set to any positive value starting with "_1".

FFIILLEESS
